# PRD template — the "__NS__" loop

*The `/intent-__NS__` minter starts every work-item from this skeleton. It is the
prose half of the contract (**why** + **what** "done" means); the companion
`run-prd-test.sh` is the executable half (the oracle). Edit this template to shape
how this loop's work-items read — including the `## Loop conventions` steering,
which is THE place namespace behavior is encoded (there is no eager namespace
memory; the agent learns "how to behave in this loop" from this section + the phase
skills).*

## Minimal skeleton (always)

```markdown
# PRD: <feature>

## Loop conventions (__NS__)
<The behavioral steering for this loop — what an agent working a __NS__ item must
do differently from a plain feature build. e.g. for a `bug` loop: "Reproduce first
with a failing check; make the smallest change that fixes it; do not expand scope
beyond the repro." This rides in every work-item and is read by the whole chain.>

## Why
<The need, in user terms. Who is hurting and how. Not the solution.>

## User story
As a <user>, I can <observable action> so that <outcome>.

## Definition of done
`./prds/<feature>/run-prd-test.sh` exits 0:
- <observable criterion 1 — what's true from outside when this is built>
- <observable criterion 2>
(Each bullet maps to a check in the runner. The runner is hard + judge-capable;
the LLM-judge legs grade against `loops/__NS__/oracle.md`. Behavioral, not
implementation-bound.)

## Out of scope
- <explicitly excluded thing — keeps the chain from drifting>

## Constraints
- <convention this must honor, sourced from the Expert>
```

## Optional blocks (add only when the conversation raises them)

```markdown
## Inputs / References
- `prds/<feature>/<contract-or-fixture>` — <copied in because the runner needs it>

## Non-functional requirements
- <perf / latency / accessibility / security target, stated observably>
```

## Notes
- **Definition of done bullets are behavioral.** Verifiable from outside, without
  reading the implementation.
- Keep `## Loop conventions` tight and namespace-specific. If a convention is
  really project-wide (not loop-specific), it belongs in the Expert / AGENTS.md,
  not here.
